  </table>

  <p>For females, the age gradient is much flatter. While the highest-risk group for women is also 25–29 (mean 3.2 per 100,000), the difference between the most and least vulnerable age groups is far smaller than for men. This reflects the different typologies of violence: male homicide victimization is strongly linked to street violence and organized crime, which disproportionately affect young men; female victimization is more often linked to intimate partner violence, which spans a wider age range.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Policy Implication:</strong> The concentration of male homicide risk in the 15–29 age bracket makes this demographic the highest-priority target for violence prevention programs. Interventions addressing youth unemployment, gang desistance, and community-based conflict resolution could yield outsized returns.
  </div>
</section>

