<!-- SECTION 6: DATA LIMITATIONS -->
<section id="data-limitations-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Data Limitations & Policy Recommendations</h2>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>

  <p>This analysis must be interpreted within several important methodological constraints:</p>

  <div class="chart-container" style="height: 300px">
    <canvas id="coverageChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <ol style="margin: 12px 0 12px 24px;">
    <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> The number of reporting countries varies substantially by year — from 84 in 2023 to 162 at peak coverage in 2008. Year-to-year trend comparisons therefore reflect both genuine changes and composition effects as different countries enter and exit the dataset.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting bias:</strong> Countries with weaker institutional capacity — often those with higher actual homicide rates — are less likely to report consistently to the UNODC. This creates a potential downward bias in global averages.</li>
    <li><strong>United Kingdom caveat:</strong> UK data in this dataset refer to England and Wales only. Scotland and Northern Ireland may appear as separate entries.</li>
    <li><strong>Territories vs. sovereign states:</strong> Territories like Hong Kong, Macao, and Puerto Rico appear as separate entries. Care should be taken not to double-count with parent countries.</li>
    <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing data were not interpolated. The absence of a row means no data was available — not that the rate was zero.</li>
    <li><strong>Sex- and age-disaggregated data:</strong> Gender and age breakdowns are available for a smaller subset of countries and years. Conclusions about demographic patterns are based on this subset and may not generalize to non-reporting nations.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>

  <ol style="margin: 12px 0 12px 24px;">
    <li><strong>Target the 15–29 male demographic:</strong> With homicide rates for males in this age bracket 20–30 times higher than for children or the elderly, violence prevention programs should prioritize youth engagement, employment, and community-based interventions.</li>
    <li><strong>Address Caribbean and Central American hotspots:</strong> The persistent concentration of the world's highest homicide rates in this region calls for enhanced international cooperation, transnational crime interdiction, and localized community safety programs.</li>
    <li><strong>Strengthen global reporting systems:</strong> The decline in reporting coverage from 162 countries in 2008 to 84 in 2023 undermines the ability to track progress. Investment in national statistical capacity — particularly in Africa and parts of Asia — should be a priority.</li>
    <li><strong>Learn from success cases:</strong> Countries like Estonia, Singapore, and Tajikistan that achieved 85%+ reductions offer potential models for evidence-based policy transfer, accounting for contextual differences.</li>
    <li><strong>Sustain gender-sensitive analysis:</strong> The stable 3–4x male-to-female ratio masks very different violence typologies. Policies should address both the street/gang violence driving male victimization and the intimate partner violence driving female victimization.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

